/* ⛔ THE SELECTOR HERE IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE.
     The leading-trim block at the head of this file is `:is(button,a,label,span,…,
     input[type=text],…):not(:has(svg))`. :is() takes the specificity of its MOST SPECIFIC
     argument — `input[type=text]` = (0,1,1) — and :not(:has(svg)) adds (0,0,1), so that
     rule lands at (0,1,2) and BEATS any single class (0,1,0). A bare `.tr-label
     {text-box-edge:text text}` therefore LOSES, the computed edge stays `cap alphabetic`,
     and every descender is clipped by the overflow:hidden the ellipsis needs.
     MEASURED in Chromium: clientHeight 12 vs scrollHeight 18 = 6px of glyph gone.
     `.tr-row > .tr-label` is (0,2,0) and wins: clientHeight 21 == scrollHeight 21, edge
     computes to `text`, ellipsis intact, row height unchanged at 44px.
     ⚠ _validate_descender_clip.py PASSES BOTH FORMS — it reads the declaration, not the
     cascade. Sidebar-nav's .sn-label carries the losing form today and is clipped in the
     same way (measured: clientHeight 12 / scrollHeight 18, edge `cap alphabetic`). That is
     a corpus finding and a gate finding, both declared in the #210 lane-A receipt. This
     lane did not edit Sidebar-nav — it is gated. */
